Description: dcc-send: split sized stream frames instead of re-keying the encoder
 When the guest draws a video frame together with something next to it (a
 counter, a progress bar), spice-server sends it as a "sized" stream frame at
 its own, bigger size. Encoders that restart on a size change (GStreamer's, for
 H.264 a new SPS and a key frame) then restart twice: to the bigger size and
 back with the next frame. With a counter changing once a second next to the
 video: two H.264 key frames a second, about 5 times a normal frame each, +28%
 of the stream in our test (more for less animated content).
 For such encoders, encode the stream's area of the frame at the stream's size
 as a normal frame, and send the rest of the drawable (within its clip) as
 images right after it (pipe_add_tail(): the next messages, before any later
 drawing). MJPEG, which encodes every frame on its own, keeps sized frames.
 Anything else (scaling, a mask, a palette, more than 8 rects, an encoder that
 refuses the frame) falls back to the sized frame as before. Clients without
 the SIZED_STREAM cap get the split frame too instead of a lossless drawable.
Author: spice-mac local build
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-10-22
